diff --git a/Servex/agents/doc/lifecycle.md b/Servex/agents/doc/lifecycle.md
index a7aec1cbc..bf92771bd 100644
--- a/Servex/agents/doc/lifecycle.md
+++ b/Servex/agents/doc/lifecycle.md
@@ -20,7 +20,7 @@ A running Servex needs `node Servex/sustain.mjs --restart` to load this.
 
 **The compaction policy for long-running agents, as it stands:** never mid-task. Node nudges an idle agent with its context size, and the agent decides whether to compact (above). The only forced `/compact` is the 400k safety ceiling, and it runs at a turn's end. On top of that, a standing agent gets a **fresh session about once a day**, so its context starts small instead of being compacted again and again.
 
-- **Renewal on wake replaces the daily respawn (2026-10-23).** A `roles.js` row with `renew: {hours, tokens}` (today only the singleton `ai-system-mastermind`) is never respawned on a timer. When a message wakes it and its session is that old or that big, it starts as a fresh session under the same id instead of resuming (`Agent.renew_due()` / `renew()` for a dormant one, `Agents.wake()` for a stopped one). Nothing runs while it is unused. It logs a `renew {why, replaces, session}` event. A row with `singleton: true` has one id, the role word: a second `spawn` of the role while it is live sends it the prompt (`Agents.spawn`). Test: `node Servex/agents/singleton.test.mjs`.
+- **No renewal, no singleton (2026-10-25).** The daily respawn was replaced by renewal on wake on 2026-10-23, and renewal is gone too: the AI system mastermind is an ordinary `mastermind` spawned at `public/framework/ai/` (`roles.js` `ALIAS_PATHS`), a fresh agent per project, reused only while its context is small. A second `spawn` is a second agent (`mastermind-ai-2`). Test: `node Servex/agents/path-mastermind.test.mjs`.
 - **Who (Respawn):** `Respawn.AGENTS` in `Servex/agents/Respawn.js` is empty since then; the class stays for an agent that must be fresh on a timer.
 - **When:** the TaskLoop tick (every 5 minutes) checks each one. Its session must be 24 hours old or more (`SERVEX_RESPAWN_HOURS`). It must not be working, nothing it spawned may be working, and nothing may wait in its queue. One stopped on purpose (someone named in `stopped_by`) stays stopped; one the Lifecycle reaper stopped because its task landed names nobody, so it is respawned. One never spawned is not spawned here.
 - **How:** stop the old session, then spawn a fresh one under the **same id**. Messages, the processes it created and the requests addressed to it still reach it. Its first prompt names the session it replaces and points at its task log and the maps.
